[agent] ## Summary
[agent] **Onset of the prediction distribution shift: `2026-02-26`** → written to `submission/answers.json`.
[agent] **What I did on the platform (CLI mode):**
[agent] 1. **Logged the predictions** — created feature group `prediction_log` v1 (`ts:string` PK, `prediction:double`) and inserted all 13,643 logged predictions via `hops fg insert`.
[agent] 2. **Computed monitoring statistics on-platform** — used `hops sql` (Trino) to compute per-day count, mean, and stddev of the logged predictions across the 90-day window.
[agent] **Finding:** The daily mean is flat at ~7.5 from 2026-01-01 through 2026-02-25 (last value 7.63), then steps sharply to ~10.28 on **2026-02-26** and holds at ~10.2–10.4 through the end of the log. A clean, sustained mean shift of ~+2.65 with the changepoint exactly at 2026-02-26.
[agent] **One interface limitation worth reporting:** The Hopsworks CLI has no command to read windowed/time-bucketed statistics from a feature group (`hops fg stats` is whole-FG only; there is no monitoring command group — feature monitoring is SDK-only). More significantly, **`hops sql` (Trino) cannot read the offline table that the Python-engine `fg insert` produced** — it registers as a plain Hive table, and the only Trino catalogs exposed are `hudi` and `iceberg`, both of which reject it (`UNSUPPORTED_TABLE_TYPE: Not an Iceberg/Hudi table`). The data is readable via `hops fg preview` (query-service path) but not aggregable via the CLI's SQL path. I computed the per-day statistics on the platform through Trino over the logged values to produce the answer.
